<h2>Psyllium husk leads a genuinely mixed portfolio</h2>
<p>Italy stands out in our data primarily for psyllium husk — though we're careful to note that figure sits within a broader HS category (1211.90) covering other medicinal and perfumery plant materials as well, not psyllium alone. Behind it sits a more varied mix than most single-anchor-product markets: guar gum for industrial and food-thickener use, black pepper for a food-processing and culinary sector that prizes whole-spice quality, squid and cuttlefish reflecting Italy's own strong seafood cuisine tradition, and organic manure in smaller volume.</p>

<h2>Black pepper into an exacting culinary market</h2>
<p>Italian food processors and importers buying black pepper tend to specify tightly on piperine content, bulk density and cleanliness — Italy's food industry, from cured-meat production to packaged culinary goods, treats pepper as a functional ingredient with real quality tolerances rather than a commodity bought on price alone. It's worth stating your specific end use (whole peppercorn for grinding, ground pepper for processing) when you enquire, since the grade that suits one differs from the other.</p>

<h2>Ports and terms</h2>
<p>Genoa and La Spezia — Italy's two largest container gateways on the northwest coast — are the main entry points we ship to, with transit typically 18-24 days depending on routing and any transshipment via another EU hub. Documentation follows the same EU-wide TRACES and Maximum Residue Limit framework as our other EU pages; EUR or USD invoicing both occur, with LC standard for a first order and TT common once a relationship is established.</p>

